Z||000006||Bluebottlejunky||07-17-2001||09:51 PM||||Look sweet man!, that turbo is gunna rock!  <BR>ps. you had a very nice car at Dells Run.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000007||sluppy123||07-17-2001||11:29 PM||||I'm using a Rising rate Fuel pressure regulator from holley. Dennis' car used a custom burned prom chip.<BR> A friend had the reg laying around so I'm going to try it first, Holley says it's good to 8 lbs of boost, so we'll see what happens. For timing I'm using a MSD Boost timing master.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||07-18-2001||10:45 AM||||Sluppy,<P>Dennis's turbo project has always interested me. Keep track of the $$$ and keep us posted on your progress. I've got a 3.4 that's just itch'n to be blown!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000009||Joe Torma||07-18-2001||10:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>  Yeah, thats cool and all, but then you will have no idle quality</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>It ran a bit rich if I remember...I think we were tearing in the garage.<P>Who wants to idle when you've got a turbo anyway?!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jaei Sun||07-18-2001||11:17 AM||||How about this intercooler??<P>You could try it looks compact enough.  It's from a Mitsu 3kgt/Stealth<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.mn3s.org/intercooler1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mr.Frosty||07-18-2001||12:19 PM||||I'm going with the boost dependant rising rate fuel pressure reg. too.Vortech also makes one for turbo/supercharged apps. too.Let me know how the install goes.Right now I'm putting the plumbing together for the intercooler.I'm using one from a T-bird turbocoupe.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled<p>[This message has been edited by Mr.Frosty (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||07-18-2001||12:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jaei Sun:<BR><B>How about this intercooler?? You could try it looks compact enough. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Actually, it looks kind of big to me... but the flexible duct would help the install...<P>Oh, wait, I was looking at the clothes dryer... never mind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000013||West Coast Fiero||07-18-2001||01:19 PM||||why not keep the stock fuel equipment, but rather have 2 extra 2.8 injectors fire into the air stream at WOT. This would work off of a 2 BAR map sensor and a pressure switch. That is roughly how we are dealing with our T-3 Turbocharged 3.4 DOHC here at the shop||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfixit58||07-18-2001||02:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jaei Sun:<BR><B>How about this intercooler?? You could try it looks compact enough.  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.mn3s.org/intercooler1.jpg">  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>That dryer must ROCK!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Joe Torma||07-18-2001||04:00 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>why not keep the stock fuel equipment, but rather have 2 extra 2.8 injectors fire into the air stream at WOT. This would work off of a 2 BAR map sensor and a pressure switch. That is roughly how we are dealing with our T-3 Turbocharged 3.4 DOHC here at the shop</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmm...something better...GARDEN HOSE INJECTION!  Just pipe it in and put a quarter turn valve on the gas petal and WOOOOSH!<P>Just bustin'...  You really need to change the pulsewidth curve on the injectors, not just dump more fuel in at WOT.<P>I hope when the vacuum drops you won't have all this fuel waiting for the turbo to spool some air to atomize it.<P>What you need to do is adjust the injector pulsewidth vs. MAP curves.  Then adjust the Air/Fuel enrichemnts to compensate.  Unfortnately, you can't do much with the stock computer.<P>So I think adding fuel over the whole range is probably better than dumping it in at WOT.  <P><BR>Joe <P><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mr.Frosty||07-18-2001||04:14 PM||||My old setup used an extra injector connected to a pressure switch to dump fuel in the intake at WOT,that just doesn't cut it.A rising rate regulator,maybe with slightly larger injectors and a high volume (Corvette) fuel pump seems to be the best way to go.Noone wants to reprogram our Fiero computers and those that do want way too much money to do it.It is just as effective to use a boost sensitive regulator,that is what they are designed to do,and they step up gradually with boost pressure.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled<p>[This message has been edited by Mr.Frosty (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||West Coast Fiero||07-18-2001||04:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>  Hmmm...something better...GARDEN HOSE INJECTION!  Just pipe it in and put a quarter turn valve on the gas petal and WOOOOSH!<P>Just bustin'...  You really need to change the pulsewidth curve on the injectors, not just dump more fuel in at WOT.<P>I hope when the vacuum drops you won't have all this fuel waiting for the turbo to spool some air to atomize it.<P>What you need to do is adjust the injector pulsewidth vs. MAP curves.  Then adjust the Air/Fuel enrichemnts to compensate.  Unfortnately, you can't do much with the stock computer.<P>So I think adding fuel over the whole range is probably better than dumping it in at WOT.  <P><BR>Joe <P>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-18-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The simplicity though of doing it the way I said is that it DOES NOT add fuel until you add boost, which is exactly what you want. Also by using the 2 BAR map sensor and the pressure switch, plus minor electronic add ons provide a steady increase from 3 mil seconds to 8 mil seconds as boost increases - so yes, it does function as the way you say a proper system should<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||00lE||07-18-2001||05:45 PM||||You may want to look into using an intercooler from an 85-93 Saab 900.  It should mount directly under the drivers side cover grill if you were to remove the air cleaner assembly and vapor canistor. Once you add some side scoops and do a little creative ducting, it should also be quite efficient.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/i-1_3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||West Coast Fiero||07-18-2001||06:14 PM||||how do you plan to draw air through the intercooler if placed under the louver||||reg||1
Z||000020||Joe Torma||07-18-2001||09:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B> Also by using the 2 BAR map sensor and the pressure switch, plus minor electronic add ons provide a steady increase from 3 mil seconds to 8 mil seconds as boost increases - so yes, it does function as the way you say a proper system should<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ah, I like that...sounds decent.  Have you tried it yet?<P>Joe<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||West Coast Fiero||07-18-2001||10:13 PM||||its been in use on our 3.4 DOHC Garrett T3 Turbo 1986 GT Coupe for the past 8 months with no hiccups<P> For questions or comments related to this subject, email or call Chris West at cwest@cwestco.com or 310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000022||BV MotorSports||07-18-2001||11:08 PM||||Dont have apic but an IC from a Daytona Turbo (or other ic'd shelby cars) have small and very efficient IC's for there compact size. It would be best to mount behind one of the current "ram-air" scoops in a sealed manner to force air thru instead of around the IC. Even a small elec fan would help radiate heat. That SABB IC looks promising too.<P>Steven<BR>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||3
Z||000023||sluppy123||07-18-2001||11:42 PM||||Well the past 2 days I didn't get much done, It's just to damn HOT, I need a A/C in the garage!! I did get the oil pan reinstalled, removed it to weld on a fitting for the oil return line from the turbo. also finished plumbing all the oil lines. I also recieved a few packages from the UPS guy, I now have all the things I need to finish the install, minus the Intercooler.<P>Hey JAEI SUN- you want to get rid of that Intercooler? I'm sure I could take it off your hands, looks like it's just in the way. What size are the inlet and outlet?||||reg||1
Z||000024||00lE||07-18-2001||11:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>how do you plan to draw air through the intercooler if placed under the louver</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Draw air??? LOL<BR>Aftermarket side scoops would supply the air flow which is directed to the bottom of the intercooler.  The air flow then travels through the intercooler and out the top where it exits through the drivers side engine compartment vent cover (directly above the intercooler).  The dimensions of the Saab intercooler are such that it would actually mount to the bottom of an engine compartment vent.  Some shrouding created from sheet metal could be used to create a sort of box to isolate the incoming air stream from the side scoop and direct it up through the intercooler. ||||reg||1
Z||000025||West Coast Fiero||07-20-2001||12:35 PM||||to it seems that you would run into stagnant air ( null air movement ) seeing as how there just is not enough air movement behind the fender well to make an intercooler efficient. The heat tranfer co-effiecient of air is increased when placed stagnant space, however, I think putting the intercooler down there would be perfect with an electric fan and some duct work to draw air up through the intercooler, through the engine bay sheet metal, and up through the louver. I think this is a mod that would flat out, be pretty damn bitchen.<P><BR>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||00lE||07-20-2001||03:41 PM||||I have already thought about the fan idea, not sure if there would be enough room tho.  It really depends on just how much air the side scoops would divert to the intercooler, and of course they would have to be the side panel replacement type (not a holley).<BR>If the side scoop does divert enough air, then stagnant air would not be an issue.  Stock intercooler applications that mount in the front of vehicles such as the eclipse turbo or Saab 900, are not fan cooled.<BR>One also should consider that the Saab intercooler is a double pass unit, so it actually has the equivlant cooling capacity of a unit twice its size.  I considered these issues when I researched this idea for my application, and it is the best I could come up with.  Do I get a free set of poly-transmission mounts for providing you with  this info?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000027||sluppy123||07-21-2001||12:12 AM||||I've got a couple questions for anybody that already has a turbo or is installing one(West coast, Mr Frosty, ect...)  <BR>1. I read somewhere that a check valve needs to be installed somewhere on one of the vac lines on top of the vapor canister. Any one know why It's needed? and if so where to get the valve?<P>2. What about the rest of the vac lines? any thing need to be done to them?<P>West coast are you running a intercooler? do you any pictures of the install of the turbo?||||reg||1
Z||000028||GT Bastard||07-21-2001||11:13 AM||||Sluppy, where did you weld the oil return fitting on the oil pan for the turbo?  On the side of the engine closest to the trunk?||||reg||1
Z||000029||sluppy123||07-21-2001||11:53 AM||||I put it on the same side as the starter, in between the starter and the oil filter.||||reg||1
Z||000030||sluppy123||07-22-2001||11:27 PM||||*BUMP*<BR>Anyone know about the vac lines?||||reg||1
Z||000031||Mr.Frosty||07-23-2001||09:53 AM||||Sorry Sluppy,I haven't heard anything about the check valve,but it does seem to make sense.I've wondered what happens to the vac lines when there is positive pressure also.Anybody?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000032||BOILERMAKER||07-23-2001||11:34 AM||||Sluppy,  it looks like you have the same turbo that I am getting ready to fab up.  I was looking it over yesterday and I have 4 ports (I know 2 are for oil, figured the other 2 were for water).  Do you have the same ports?<P>If so, i was wanting to find a diagram or pic that shows which is oil in/out and which is water in/out.  If you (or anybody else) has info, could you share with me?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Mr.Frosty||07-23-2001||11:45 AM||||Mine is a water cooled turbo also.I haven't looked in a while,but I'm pretty sure the water lines run in from the sides and the oil lines go in from the top and bottom.The oil is gravity fed through the turbo down through the line to the pan where it drains back,since the water is pressure fed it feeds through the sides into the water jacket.Hope that helps.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000034||sluppy123||07-23-2001||11:51 PM||||My turbo only has 2 fittings, for the oil in and out, I was looking for a liquid cooled one but found this one first. Mr Frosty is right about the fittings, The oil fittings are top and bottom and water on the sides.<P>Here is a pic of the install as it sits now.   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=13494839&p=52071710&Sequence=0">  <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=13494839&p=52071711&Sequence=0"> <P>So far I have all the exhaust and intake plumbing done. All the oil lines are hooked up, the adj fuel pressure regulator has been installed. I still need to install the rising rate regulator, gauges and MSD BTM ignition. <P>As soon as I find out about the Vac lines I'll fire it up and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000035||00lE||07-24-2001||12:02 PM||||Dont forget to replace the regular fuel (if you were using it prior to the turbo instal) with some good premium fuel, maybe even a bottle of octane booster just as a precaution. :-)||||reg||1
Z||000036||Jaei Sun||07-24-2001||11:29 PM||||Sorry Sluppy123, that turbo intercooler used to belong to my friend and he sold it two weeks ago.. but they go for around $90 or so.||||reg||14
